From: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: rts5453: replace shared-IRQ workaround with SMBus ARA dispatch, add debugfs diagnostics, and fix audit-flagged correctness/lifetime issues

MS-R1 wires two RTS5453 PD controllers to the same physical IRQ line
on a shared I2C adapter, each described in ACPI as its own
(incorrectly) "Exclusive" GpioInt. The previous approach elected one
i2c_client as a permanent "owner" of the shared IRQ, which then polled
its sibling on every interrupt via a manual sibling-list walk
(rts_list/rts_lock, rts5453h_irq_rts_handle()). This works but is
fragile: it depends on election ordering, cannot use the standard
threaded-IRQ/PM-wake machinery per device, and does not scale cleanly
to N shared devices.

Replace it with the standard SMBus Alert Response Address (ARA)
mechanism (SMBus 2.0 Alert Response protocol, already fully supported
by this kernel's i2c-smbus core), then harden the result against a
follow-up correctness/lifetime review (patchset_20260728b audit) that
found several release-blocking issues in an earlier version of this
change. Both the original ARA redesign and the audit fixes are folded
into this single patch (originally split as 0078/0079 core+debugfs;
consolidated here because the intermediate states are not
independently useful/bisectable) - the 0079 slot is intentionally left
unused going forward, consistent with the numbering gaps already
present in the upstream CIX vendor patch series.

Design (SMBus ARA IRQ group):

 - A new struct rts5453_irq_group is created per (i2c_adapter, Linux
   IRQ) pair the first time an RTS5453 device on that adapter/IRQ
   probes, and is refcounted across all devices that share it.
 - The physical IRQ is *not* requested by this driver at all. The
   group instead passes struct i2c_smbus_alert_setup{ .irq =
   group->irq } (a non-zero IRQ) to i2c_new_smbus_alert_device(),
   which makes the generic SMBus-alert core's own smbalert_probe()
   request the IRQ itself, threaded, with the synchronous bounded ARA
   drain loop as its handler body (smbus_alert() in i2c-smbus.c: reads
   the ARA repeatedly, decodes the responding address, dispatches to
   the matching i2c_client's .alert() via smbus_do_alert(), and stops
   once no client claims the address). This is deliberately different
   from an earlier draft of this change, which called the
   asynchronous i2c_handle_smbus_alert() (which only schedules
   ara->work and returns) from a driver-owned, separately-requested
   IRQF_ONESHOT thread; on an active-low level IRQ that meant the
   group's own thread returned - and the line was unmasked - before
   the ARA was ever actually read, allowing the still-asserted line to
   retrigger immediately and IRQ-storm. Letting the generic core own
   the IRQ end-to-end avoids this entirely, and also preserves
   whatever trigger type ACPI/GpioInt configured (verified by reading
   i2c-smbus.c: the non-GPIO-fallback IRQ request path adds no
   trigger-type flags of its own). irq_get_trigger_type() is checked
   at group-start time and a dev_warn() is logged if it is not
   IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW, so a misconfigured platform is visible in dmesg
   instead of silently storming.
 - Only the specific address that responds to an ARA read is
   serviced - no sibling polling, no election.
 - Status capture (rts5453_capture_status_locked(), shared by both the
   interrupt-driven and initial/restore-time paths so they cannot
   diverge) is split from status application
   (rts5453_apply_status()/rts5453_apply_work()), with change-detection
   guards on typec_switch_set()/typec_mux_set().
 - I2C errors are no longer collapsed to a single -ESHUTDOWN value;
   the original errno is preserved end to end
   (rts5453_capture_and_clear_alert(), rts->last_capture_error). Only
   -ENODEV/-ENXIO/-ESHUTDOWN are treated as immediately "permanent"
   (offline); any other error escalates to offline only after
   RTS5453_OFFLINE_ERROR_THRESHOLD (16) consecutive capture failures,
   so a single transient -EAGAIN/-EREMOTEIO/timeout no longer
   permanently disables the port.
 - rts5453h_typec_port_update()'s cached typec->role is now updated
   only *after* usb_role_switch_set_role() succeeds (it was previously
   set unconditionally beforehand), so a failed switch no longer wedges
   an incorrect cached role that would prevent an identical retry.
 - apply_work is now a delayed_work: a failed apply is retried up to
   RTS5453_APPLY_MAX_RETRIES (5) times with RTS5453_APPLY_RETRY_DELAY
   (50 ms) backoff before rts->faulted is latched and logged (these are
   software retry-policy constants, not derived from any hardware
   timing requirement - no arbitrary hardware delay was invented).
   rts5453_apply_status() now returns an error instead of void, and
   rts5453_apply_work() propagates/logs it via a shared
   rts5453_log_context()/rts5453_log_context_throttled() helper that
   dumps port id, adapter/address, Linux IRQ, operation, errno, all six
   raw status bytes, cached mode/hpd/orientation/role, pending/applied
   generation and consecutive-error counts (throttled to every
   occurrence for the first 3 consecutive failures, then every 16th,
   so the first failure and each state transition are never dropped).
 - rts5453_read_initial_status() (probe/restore) now shares the same
   checked capture+apply helpers as the interrupt path instead of its
   own separate, less-checked update logic, and no longer risks
   unregistering a NULL Type-C partner.
 - The group, not each member device, now owns IRQ enable/disable and
   wake-enable/disable state
   (rts5453_group_irq_disable()/_enable()/_wake_enable()/_wake_disable(),
   mutex+depth-counted under group->pm_lock), called from
   rts5453h_suspend()/_resume()/_restore()/_shutdown() instead of each
   device independently calling disable_irq()/enable_irq() on the
   shared physical IRQ. This is more robust across partial-probe,
   single-member-remove, shutdown and PM-callback-failure orderings
   than relying on the interrupt core's own per-irq_desc depth
   counting composing correctly by coincidence. The pre-existing
   disable_pd_interrupt_store() sysfs knob had a latent bug where it
   called disable_irq()/enable_irq() unconditionally on every store
   regardless of whether the value actually changed, which would have
   desynchronized this depth accounting; it is now gated on an actual
   value transition.
 - Group teardown (rts5453_group_leave(), last member) is just
   i2c_unregister_device(group->ara_client): unbinding the ARA client
   from smbalert_driver runs devres_release_all() -> free_irq() on the
   IRQ smbalert_probe() requested via devm_request_threaded_irq(),
   and free_irq() blocks until any in-flight threaded-handler
   invocation completes - so no separate manual disable_irq()/
   synchronize_irq()/free_irq() sequence is needed to fully quiesce
   dispatch before the group is freed.
 - A member device unregistering while an ARA dispatch for that same
   device's address is in flight is already race-free without any
   extra driver-side locking: smbus_do_alert() takes
   device_lock(&client->dev) before checking client->dev.driver and
   calling .alert(), and the i2c/driver-core unbind path
   (i2c_unregister_device() -> device_release_driver_internal(),
   triggered by our own rts5453h_remove()) takes that same per-device
   lock around calling .remove(), only clearing dev->driver at the
   very end while still holding it - so a device's own .remove() and
   its own .alert() can never run concurrently.
 - rts5453h_probe() is converted to labelled goto cleanup
   (err_group_leave/err_unregister_ports) so a late failure (group
   join, sysfs attribute creation) unwinds previously-registered
   Type-C port/altmode/switch/mux/role-switch resources instead of
   returning directly and leaking them. A pre-existing bug is also
   fixed here: a failure from rts5453h_init_ports() was previously
   coerced unconditionally into -EPROBE_DEFER, which would have turned
   a permanent failure (e.g. -EINVAL from bad port properties) into an
   indefinitely-retried deferred probe; the real error code is now
   returned (rts5453h_init_ports() already unwinds anything it
   partially registered on its own failure paths, so no further
   cleanup is needed for that specific case).

Debugfs diagnostics (read-only, added on top of the above, no
behavior-affecting code):

 - /sys/kernel/debug/rts5453/<adapter>-irq<N>/topology - group members,
   their I2C addresses and the observed IRQ trigger type.
 - .../counters - group-level IRQ disable/wake depth counters (the
   old irq_count/ara_runs/ara_dispatch_errors counters are gone: with
   the generic core now owning ARA dispatch end-to-end, this driver no
   longer observes those events directly).
 - .../member-0x%02x - per-device counters, including separate
   attempt/success pairs for orientation, mux and role-switch
   operations (so a debugfs snapshot can distinguish "operation was
   attempted and failed" from "operation was never attempted"),
   fault/offline state, and last-error/consecutive-error fields for
   both capture and apply.

The debugfs root is created/destroyed from new explicit module_init()/
module_exit() functions (replacing module_i2c_driver()), and the
per-group/per-member directories and files are created and torn down
alongside the corresponding group/device lifecycle. All debugfs
creation is unchecked by design: debugfs_create_*() calls safely no-op
against a NULL/ERR_PTR parent, so a failure here is diagnostics-only
and never affects driver functionality. This kernel's debugfs
implementation (DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE + debugfs_create_file(), not the
"_unsafe" variant) already fully serializes debugfs_remove() against
any in-flight reader via wait_for_completion(&active_users_drained),
so no additional driver-side reference-counting scheme is needed to
make these files safe to remove while open.

